    "q-140587": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140587",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $y<x+3?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we draw the corresponding straight line $y=x+3.$ Since the inequality involves a 'strictly less than' sign, the line $y=x+3$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **dashed line.**\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14274623/assets/8e1b659099f0fa292d9cb643abf902633a152662bd756ad89041cc43600ab0d1.png)\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality $y<x+3$ refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line $y=x+3,$ so we shade *below* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14274623/assets/21c052085b500607ae2a44c8a154035e34a20c76092d2da57d5d07f8c37e650a.png)\n\nLet's now check that we've shaded the correct part. First, we consider a point in the shaded region, say $(0,1).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14274623/assets/1ea5248177b9ab51e31b87c231684d3317d2d7c564a33a620d9826c1152e0772.png)\n\nWhen we substitute the coordinates of this point into our inequality, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & <x+3 \\\\ (1) & <(0)+3 \\\\ 1 & <3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis indicates that we have shaded the correct region.",
