    "q-215162": {
      "dom_id": "step-q215162",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14274623/assets/7e693ed228592f5a859a653811d466f8f65c4af96dcc59700ebf76f6d1dd46d3.png)\n\nWhat inequality describes the shaded region shown above?\n\n$y$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the equation of the corresponding line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$\n\n- The line intersects the $y$-axis at $(0,4)$, so the $y$-intercept is $b=4.$\n\n- The line passes through the points $(0,4)$ and $(2,2)$, so its slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{2-4}{2-0}=\\frac{-2}{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=-x+4.$\n\nFinally, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is dashed, we will use a strict inequality. Since the shaded region is *below* the line, the inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line.\n\nSo, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<-x+4.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
